       DirectedInfinity[1]]]},
    {
     PaneBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"Figure 3. The same Alcubierre bubble flying across the \
frame at v = c.  The colourmap shows Eulerian energy density; the black \
circle is the nominal bubble radius R = 1.  Notice that the bubble geometry \
is rigidly carried along \[LongDash] no shockwaves, no wakes \[LongDash] \
precisely because the construction is engineered to be flat inside and \
outside.\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontFamily->"Helvetica",
       FontSize->10,
       FontSlant->Italic],
      Alignment->Center,
      ImageSize->720]}
   },
   DefaultBaseStyle->"Column",
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{0.6}}}],
  "Column"]], "Output",
 CellMargins->{{20, 20}, {10, 14}},
 ShowCellLabel->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"57e5272d-9ae2-45dd-b8e9-07d582e8adae"],

Cell["5b. Putting a spacecraft inside the bubble", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"386d6f6b-4da9-48a0-bbd0-6cfb8d05ba4e"],

Cell["\<\
None of the geometry above changes if we put a \"ship\" inside the bubble \
\[LongDash] in Alcubierre's metric the interior is exactly flat Minkowski, so \
the spacecraft is in inertial free-fall and feels no acceleration even as the \
bubble accelerates across the galaxy.  This makes for a striking \
visualisation, and because the package builds the metric symbolically we can \
render a fully self-consistent scene: the warp surface (a height field built \
from the shape function and the shift component), the translucent bubble, the \
magenta exotic-matter ring at the equator, the spacecraft mesh, an engine \
wake, and a star field.  The camera orbits the bubble while travelling with \
it, so the spacecraft stays centred while the stars stream past:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"05cf68fd-954d-4051-aab7-d2b02d03075b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The spacecraft itself is a small ",
 StyleBox["GraphicsComplex", "Input"],
 " with 17 vertices and 30 triangular faces, plus two flat-quad nacelles \
drawn in glowing blue.  The mesh is a literal port of the Python ",
 StyleBox["create_spacecraft()", "Input"],
 " in ",
 StyleBox["generate_hero_animation.py", "Input"],
 ":"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"afd6c911-3abd-4b0a-93d9-690d015e8044"],

Cell["\<\
hullLength = 0.6; hullWidth = 0.15; hullHeight = 0.08;
shipVerts  = (* nose, four front, four mid (widest), four rear,
                four engine \[LongDash] 17 vertices total *)
  { {hullLength 0.6, 0, 0},
    {hullLength 0.2,  hullWidth 0.3,  hullHeight 0.5},
    {hullLength 0.2, -hullWidth 0.3,  hullHeight 0.5},
    {hullLength 0.2, -hullWidth 0.3, -hullHeight 0.5},
    {hullLength 0.2,  hullWidth 0.3, -hullHeight 0.5},
    {0,  hullWidth 0.5,  hullHeight 0.7},
    {0, -hullWidth 0.5,  hullHeight 0.7},
    {0, -hullWidth 0.5, -hullHeight 0.5},
    {0,  hullWidth 0.5, -hullHeight 0.5},
    (* ... rear + engine vertices ... *)
    {-hullLength 0.5,  hullWidth 0.25, -hullHeight 0.2}};
shipFaces = {{1,2,3},{1,3,4},{1,4,5},{1,5,2}, (* nose cone *)
             (* ... 26 more triangles ... *) };
spacecraft3D[{xc_, yc_, zc_}, scale_] :=
  {RGBColor[0.78, 0.78, 0.82], Specularity[White, 30],
   GraphicsComplex[scale # + {xc, yc, zc} & /@ shipVerts,
                   Polygon[shipFaces]],
   Glow[RGBColor[0.25, 0.5, 1]], RGBColor[0.25, 0.5, 1],
   (* nacelles ... *),
   Glow[Cyan], Cyan,
   Sphere[{xc - 0.3 scale, yc, zc}, 0.05 scale]};\
\>", "Input",
 CellTags->"runnable",ExpressionUUID->"11e52761-0ac3-47e5-bb6a-0efbd3987d17"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The full mesh definition lives in ",
 StyleBox["build_hero_assets.wls", "Input"],
 ", which also produces the GIF.  Built into a 60-frame scene with an \
orbiting camera and a starfield, the result is the cover animation reproduced \
below at full size:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"977d5eb0-579e-41bd-b673-0b62acaa47a9"],

